  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Expressed in terms of common logarithms, what value of $x$ satisfies the equation ${10}^{x-3}={7}^{x}?$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we take the common logarithm $(log)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{10}^{x-3} & ={7}^{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}\u2061({10}^{x-3}) & =\\operatorname{log}\u2061({7}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}\u2061({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3 & =\\operatorname{log}\u2061({7}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3 & =x\\operatorname{log}\u2061(7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-x\\operatorname{log}\u2061(7) & =3 \\\\ x(1-\\operatorname{log}\u2061(7)) & =3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{1-\\operatorname{log}\u2061(7)}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
